{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:595e9f97d8d6e85eb681184367245755f473bf4647df98a45fb339f631390c79","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.25,"B":0.39,"C":0.26,"D":0.1}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Lexical-priority lock applied, first match wins, using MY CQ3 (1.0 / 2.1 / 3.1, treated as approximately normal with sigma = (3.1-1.0)/2.563 = 0.82, mild right skew) and MY OECD-38 core path. Wave 1: 17/53/13/17, 9/45/24/22, 17/42/21/20. Mine: 25/39/26/10. B is no longer comfortably modal, A and C are both far above the Wave-1 centre, and D is a third of it.\n\nMY OECD-38 CORE PATH FOR 2032-36, which is the input that separates me from all three. From CQ2: on PPP weights with the imputation correction and shelter at 3.4-3.6%, 2027-2030 runs ~3.1% and 2031-2036 runs ~2.73%. So the 2032-36 central is ~2.65-2.70%, and P(core 2032-36 >= 2.7%) = 0.42, against their 0.20 / 0.25 / 0.27. I accept their front-loading shape — the impulses (grid capex crowding, electricity pass-through, the tariff LEVEL shift, the first care-wage wave) are front-loaded and partly one-off, and the disinflationary forces build — but their back-half number is too cold for exactly the reason in my CQ2: the bloc that is supposed to do the back-half disinflating is 0.08-0.12 of core and partly imputed counter-cyclically, not 0.20-0.25 of core deflating at 0-1%.\n\nSTEP 1, A (coupled growth >= 2.6%/yr): P(z >= (2.6-2.1)/0.82 = 0.61) = 0.271. Trimmed to 25 for the strictness of the threshold at the measure. DERIVED from my own CQ3, not chosen: my P50 of 2.1 does not permit most mass on A, and it does not get it — but it permits far more than the 9-17 Wave 1 assigned, and the gap is entirely the aging-drag double-count plus the unpriced participation/composition pull.\nSTEP 2, C (OECD core >= 2.7% AND growth < 2.0%): P(growth < 2.0) = Phi((2.0-2.1)/0.82) = Phi(-0.122) = 0.45. P(core >= 2.7) = 0.42. Stagflation correlation +0.30 (a supply-wall world is both hot and slow). 0.45 x 0.42 + 0.30 x sqrt(0.45 x 0.55 x 0.42 x 0.58) = 0.189 + 0.30 x 0.2456 = 0.189 + 0.074 = 0.263 -> 26. Disjoint from A on growth (>=2.6 vs <2.0), so no lexical overlap correction. Note the honest tension P1 identified and I inherit: the supply walls sit substantially OUTSIDE the measure (US core CPI/PCE and euro HICP core exclude household energy; construction enters only through rent/OER; ISIC Q prices are administered). My C is nonetheless more than double his because I am claiming the measure is hotter than he thinks for a DIFFERENT reason — the cold leg is mismeasured, not the hot leg underweighted.\nSTEP 3, D (growth < 1.7% AND core < 2.0%): P(growth < 1.7) = Phi(-0.488) = 0.313. P(core 2032-36 < 2.0%) = 0.15, which is the sharpest single disagreement in this payload (they had 0.25 / 0.25 / 0.30): getting the PPP-weighted OECD-38 aggregate under 2.0% requires the five high-inflation members at an ~11-12% PPP weight to disinflate to ~3% AND shelter to go under 2% AND the imputed financial-services lines to turn negative, which is a three-way conjunction that essentially only occurs inside my lower-tail premortem. Correlation +0.40 (one demand-deficit cause produces both). 0.313 x 0.15 + 0.40 x sqrt(0.313 x 0.687 x 0.15 x 0.85) = 0.047 + 0.40 x 0.1652 = 0.047 + 0.066 = 0.113 -> 10. Disjoint from C on inflation. I also respect P1's hard outside-view constraint: no 5-year window of OECD+China PPP growth since 1960 has printed below 1.7%, so D requires a never-before-seen outcome, and his pulling D DOWN for that reason while keeping it at 17 was half-hearted.\nSTEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 25 - 26 - 10 = 39. Sum = 100 exactly.\n\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED, as the lock demands. (i) vs CQ3: A at 25 is read off my own CQ3 right tail (P(>=2.6) = 0.27), not set independently; my P50 of 2.1 sits inside B's residual cell (growth 1.7-2.5% with core between 2.0 and 2.69%), which is why B remains the largest single cell; D's 10 is read off P(growth<1.7) = 0.31 multiplied by a core condition I hold at 0.15. (ii) vs CQ2: CQ2 = 35 is the joint of (core >= 2.50% over 2027-36, my marginal 0.72) and (China CPI < 1.50%, my 0.42). C uses a HIGHER bar (2.70%) over a LATER, SHORTER window (2032-36) and carries no China condition: dropping the China conjunct raises it, the higher bar and the back-half window lower it, and 26 sits consistently beside 35. B + C = 65, comfortably above CQ2's 35, so the hot-OECD cell is not oversubscribed. Explicitly: my P(core >= 2.50%, 2027-36) = 0.72 and my P(core >= 2.70%, 2032-36) = 0.42 are mutually consistent ONLY because of the front-loaded path (3.1% then 2.73%); if the path were flat at 2.88% then P(>=2.70% in 2032-36) would be ~0.60 and C would rise to ~33 with B falling to ~32. That is the single internal joint on which my CQ6 pivots, and it is the same pivot P2 identified — I just sit on the other side of it. (iii) The relative-price skew is still extreme in my world (electricity, construction and care at 4-6%; genuinely market-priced software and media at 0-1%); what differs from all three Wave-1 panelists is that the skew no longer nets to a dull aggregate, because the cold leg is too light and too imputed to do the netting.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.205Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.2% for three consecutive monthly BEA releases, AND the euro-area HICP core 12-month rate is at or below 2.0% in the same quarter, with neither driven by a measured recession (OECD-38 harmonized unemployment still under 6%).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my central claim that the cold leg is mismeasured and the hot legs persist. My CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.72 to ~0.45 and CQ2 drops from 35 to ~22; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~18, CQ6-B absorbs the rest (~43). Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 32 to ~60, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre. Does not move CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS and Eurostat published indices for information, professional/technical and financial services show OUTRIGHT annual price declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years, in series that are market-priced rather than cost- or asset-fee-imputed (specifically: BLS PPI for software publishers and for computer systems design, and the HICP COICOP 08.3 / professional-services sub-indices).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my strongest attack on the Wave-1 panel — that the deflatable slice is only 0.08-0.12 of core and partly counter-cyclically hot. The cold leg is then real at a 0.20-0.25 weight and I lose ~0.30 pp off my core path. CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~17, CQ6-B rises to ~46, CQ2 falls to ~26, and the Wave-1 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis is vindicated against me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO (any vintage) or the OECD Economic Outlook publishes a medium-term China real GDP growth path for 2032-2036 averaging below 2.6%/yr, or China's realized PPP-weighted real growth for calendar 2030 and 2031 both print below 3.0%, with the property-price index still falling year-on-year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central correction to Wave 1's aging-drag double-count was wrong in the direction that mattered most: the subtraction was not a double-count but a forecast of a China that does keep decelerating. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1 to ~1.75 and P90 from 3.1 to ~2.8; CQ6-A falls from 25 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~17, and P2's 1.6 P50 becomes the better number. Also lowers my CQ4 P50 by ~0.15 through the (r-g) identity.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey (2030 reference year, published ~2032) plus national equivalents show ISIC Q median gross hourly earnings growing more slowly in real terms than ISIC J+K+M over 2026-2030, in a majority of reporting OECD-38 members, with no binding statutory care-pay award enacted in Germany, Japan, the UK or France in that period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the administered-wage-jump mechanism that is the entire reason my CQ1 is 20 rather than 11. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.28 to ~0.12 and CQ1 drops from 20 to ~10, i.e. to the Wave-1 centre. Hypothesis plank (a) falls from 7 to ~4. Mildly lowers my OECD core path (care is 0.15 of core at 3.6% in my build), taking CQ2 to ~31 and CQ6-C to ~23.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Strict operational OECD-38 + China data-centre electricity consumption, on the IEA's own reporting basis, is at or below 650 TWh for calendar 2032 (against ~365 TWh in 2024), OR announced hyperscaler capacity is written down by 20%+ AND measured operational TWh falls year-on-year in any year 2029-2033.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my contrarian coupling call that AI-Q3 is TOO LOW (I say P50 ~1,050-1,100 TWh against the unanimous Wave-1 shade-down to 820-900) and specifically my claim that an impairment event impairs the accounting, not the kilowatt-hours. The electricity-pass-through leg of my hot-core path weakens, CQ2 falls to ~30 and CQ6-C to ~21; CQ3 P50 falls ~0.1 through the capital-deepening term; CQ6-D rises to ~14. The unanimous Wave-1 read would be the right one and my central coupling finding would be the ensemble's worst input.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 premortem seat, effort 'inflation'."}}